---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Hero from '~/components/widgets/Hero.astro';
import Content from '~/components/widgets/Content.astro';
import Features3 from '~/components/widgets/Features3.astro';
import CallToAction from '~/components/widgets/CallToAction.astro';

const metadata = {
  title: 'Build a Shopping Cart — Masterschool Project Walkthrough',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <Hero tagline="Mentor · Instructional Design · Loom · Masterschool · Udacity · 2022">
    <Fragment slot="title">
      Build a Shopping Cart —<br />
      <span class="text-accent dark:text-white">Step-by-Step Project Walkthrough</span>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="subtitle">
      Recorded a step-by-step Loom screencast walkthrough for Masterschool students working through the Build a Shopping Cart project on the Udacity platform — guiding them from project setup through to completion.
    </Fragment>
  </Hero>

  <!-- Context -->
  <Content
    tagline="The Project"
    title="Walking students through a real project, start to finish"
    isReversed
  >
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full py-12">
        <div class="text-center">
          <p class="text-7xl font-bold font-heading text-accent leading-none">Shopping<br/>Cart</p>
          <p class="text-xl text-muted mt-3">Masterschool · Udacity</p>
          <div class="mt-8 space-y-2 text-sm text-muted">
            <p class="font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">Mentor · Instructional Design</p>
            <p>Loom Screencast Walkthrough</p>
            <p>2022</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">Masterschool incorporated the Udacity platform into parts of their curriculum, giving students access to industry-aligned projects alongside their core bootcamp coursework. One of those projects was Build a Shopping Cart — a practical, hands-on exercise that required students to apply their development skills to a real-world feature.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">For students navigating an unfamiliar platform and tackling a project without prior context, knowing where to start and how to move through each step is its own challenge — separate from the technical skills themselves. Getting stuck at the setup stage or unsure how to approach the project structure slows learning before it even begins.</p>
      <p>I recorded a step-by-step Loom walkthrough that guided students through the full project — from initial setup to final completion — so they could follow along at their own pace, pause and rewind as needed, and work through the project with a clear path forward.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern-b"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- What the guide covered -->
  <Features3
    tagline="The Walkthrough"
    title="One guide, one project, from setup to completion"
    subtitle="The walkthrough was scoped to the full Build a Shopping Cart project — giving students a clear path from the first step to the last."
    columns={3}
    items={[
      {
        title: 'Project Setup',
        description: 'Walking students through how to access the project on the Udacity platform, understand the requirements, and get the local environment ready before writing any code.',
        icon: 'tabler:settings',
      },
      {
        title: 'Step-by-Step Build',
        description: 'A guided walkthrough of building the shopping cart feature — covering the key steps, decisions, and implementation details students needed to complete the project successfully.',
        icon: 'tabler:shopping-cart',
      },
      {
        title: 'Project Completion',
        description: 'Guidance on finishing the project, reviewing the requirements, and confirming everything is in place before submitting — reducing uncertainty around what "done" actually looks like.',
        icon: 'tabler:circle-check',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <!-- Why screencasts for project walkthroughs -->
  <Content
    tagline="The Approach"
    title="Why a screencast works better than written instructions for project work"
  >
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">Written project instructions tell students what to build. A screencast shows them how to build it. For students working through a hands-on coding project on an unfamiliar platform, seeing the full workflow in motion — the setup, the decisions, the progression from step to step — removes the ambiguity that written docs often leave behind.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">Loom walkthroughs are especially effective for project-based learning because students can pause at exactly the moment they need to catch up, replay sections they didn't fully follow, and move at their own pace without waiting for a live session.</p>
      <p>This mirrors the approach that works in workplace AI adoption training: meet people at the moment of confusion with something specific, visual, and immediately actionable — rather than documentation they have to translate into action on their own.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern-d"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- Curriculum Contribution -->
  <Content
    tagline="Curriculum Contribution"
    title="Reviewing the project and contributing changes back to Udacity"
  >
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">Working closely with this project as a mentor gave me a detailed view of the student experience — including where the project itself had gaps, unclear instructions, or issues that were creating unnecessary friction for learners. Part of my role at Masterschool extended into reviewing the curriculum materials directly and identifying improvements that could be made upstream.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">I submitted those changes to Udacity via GitHub pull requests against the official project starter repository. Both pull requests were reviewed and merged by Sarah, a curriculum manager at Udacity and Masterschool's point of contact — a direct collaboration between mentoring practice and curriculum development at the platform level.</p>
      <div class="mt-6 space-y-3">
        <a
          href="https://github.com/udacity/cd2073-intro-to-js-1-project-starter/pull/1"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="flex items-center gap-3 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 hover:border-accent dark:hover:border-accent transition-colors group"
        >
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-accent flex-shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="18" cy="18" r="3"/><circle cx="6" cy="6" r="3"/><path d="M13 6h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v7"/><path d="M6 9v12"/></svg>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm group-hover:text-accent transition-colors">Pull Request #1 — cd2073-intro-to-js-1-project-starter</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted">Reviewed and merged by Sarah, Curriculum Manager at Udacity</p>
          </div>
        </a>
        <a
          href="https://github.com/udacity/cd2073-intro-to-js-1-project-starter/pull/3"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="flex items-center gap-3 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700 hover:border-accent dark:hover:border-accent transition-colors group"
        >
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-accent flex-shrink-0" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="18" cy="18" r="3"/><circle cx="6" cy="6" r="3"/><path d="M13 6h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v7"/><path d="M6 9v12"/></svg>
          <div>
            <p class="font-semibold text-sm group-hover:text-accent transition-colors">Pull Request #3 — cd2073-intro-to-js-1-project-starter</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-muted">Reviewed and merged by Sarah, Curriculum Manager at Udacity</p>
          </div>
        </a>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern-b"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <!-- Video -->
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 md:px-8 py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="text-center mb-10">
      <p class="text-base text-secondary dark:text-blue-200 font-bold tracking-wide uppercase mb-2">Screencast Guide</p>
      <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tighter font-heading">Watch the Walkthrough</h2>
      <p class="text-lg text-muted mt-4">Step-by-step Loom guide recorded for Masterschool students working through the Build a Shopping Cart project on Udacity, 2022.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold mb-3">Build a Shopping Cart — Full Project Walkthrough</h3>
      <div class="relative w-full rounded-lg shadow-lg overflow-hidden" style="padding-top: 56.25%;">
        <iframe
          class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full"
          src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/H7ybB3nEMkY"
          title="Step-by-Step Guide: Build a Shopping Cart — Masterschool & Udacity"
          frameborder="0"
          allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture"
          allowfullscreen
        ></iframe>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Reflection -->
  <Content
    tagline="Reflection"
    title="Project walkthroughs are a core part of practical technical training"
    isReversed
  >
    <Fragment slot="image">
      <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full py-12">
        <div class="text-center">
          <p class="text-8xl font-bold font-heading text-accent leading-none">2022</p>
          <p class="text-xl text-muted mt-3">Masterschool · Udacity</p>
          <div class="mt-8 space-y-2 text-sm text-muted">
            <p class="font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">Mentor · Instructional Design</p>
            <p>Loom Screencast Walkthrough</p>
            <p>Build a Shopping Cart</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="content">
      <p class="mb-4">What this project reinforced is that learners don't just need to know what to build — they need to see how to approach it. The gap between understanding a concept and applying it to a real project is where most learners get stuck, and a step-by-step walkthrough bridges that gap in a way that written instructions rarely do.</p>
      <p class="mb-4">Recording this guide as part of my mentoring work at Masterschool was also an exercise in instructional design under real constraints: identifying the decision points where students would most likely get confused, pacing the walkthrough to be followable without being hand-held, and keeping the content focused enough to be useful in one sitting.</p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {['Loom', 'Screencast', 'Project Walkthrough', 'Technical Onboarding', 'Async Learning', 'Udacity', 'Masterschool', 'Instructional Design', 'Adult Learning', 'JavaScript', 'Curriculum Development', 'GitHub'].map(tag => (
          <span class="bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-900 text-blue-800 dark:text-blue-200 text-sm font-medium px-3 py-1 rounded-full">{tag}</span>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-4">Mentor & Instructional Design, Masterschool — Build a Shopping Cart Project Walkthrough, Udacity, 2022.</p>
    </Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="bg">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 section-pattern"></div>
    </Fragment>
  </Content>

  <CallToAction
    actions={[
      { variant: 'primary', text: 'View All Projects', href: '/projects' },
      { text: 'Get in Touch', href: '/about#contact' },
    ]}
  >
    <Fragment slot="title">Interested in working together?</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="subtitle">I'm open to remote opportunities in instructional design and technical learning experience design.</Fragment>
  </CallToAction>
</Layout>
